{
  "id": "r03",
  "text": "The official PlayStation guide describes that same bargain: examine the surroundings, listen carefully, and work out what lets you continue. It also acknowledges something less elegant: people were solving parts of P.T. through trial and error, or by working together. That uncertainty spread outside the game. Players compared clues and tried to understand which actions actually mattered. Even a contemporary Game Informer discussion asked readers whether they had managed to finish the teaser. The mystery wasn't only inside the corridor. Nobody had initially been told what this thing was advertising. At Gamescom in August twenty fourteen, it appeared under the name seven seven eight zero s Studio. The announcement gave people a horror experiment they could download immediately. It also gave them a reason to pass it around. If somebody else reached an ending you couldn't reach, their discovery might explain what you had missed. A small game acquired a much larger conversation around it, and that conversation was about to become the advertisement.",
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
